### 2\. Command-line binary manipulation tools

Essential for Ubuntu and Termux

These are especially useful because you can automate binary analysis through scripts.

| Tool    | Main function                     | Important commands |
| ------- | --------------------------------- | ------------------ |
| xxd     | Hex dump and conversion           | xxd, xxd -r        |
| hexdump | Display bytes                     | hexdump -C         |
| od      | Display data in different formats | od -An -tx1        |
| od      | Inspect binary values             | od -t x4           |
| strings | Extract readable strings          | strings -a         |
| file    | Identify file types               | file sample.bin    |
| cmp     | Compare binary files              | cmp file1 file2    |
| diff    | Compare file contents             | diff               |
| dd      | Copy and transform raw data       | dd                 |
| bvi     | Edit binary files in terminal     | bvi                |
| radiff2 | Compare binary files              | radiff2            |
| bgrep   | Search binary patterns            | bgrep              |

Example:

```bash
# Create a binary file
printf '\x41\x42\x43\x44' > sample.bin

# Inspect its hexadecimal representation
xxd sample.bin

# Inspect its byte values
hexdump -C sample.bin

# Display its strings
strings -a sample.bin

# Identify its file type
file sample.bin
```

Expected hexadecimal bytes:

```text
41 42 43 44
```

These represent ASCII characters `ABCD`.

### 3\. Python binary manipulation libraries

Essential for automation

Python is one of your most important tools because it allows you to write your own binary-analysis utilities.

| Library       | Purpose                                |
| ------------- | -------------------------------------- |
| struct        | Pack and unpack binary structures      |
| binascii      | Binary-to-hex and related conversions  |
| bitarray      | Manipulate individual bits             |
| bitstring     | Read and construct bit-level data      |
| construct     | Define and parse binary structures     |
| ctypes        | Work with C-compatible data structures |
| mmap          | Map files into memory                  |
| array         | Store typed numeric values             |
| int and bytes | Built-in binary conversions            |
| socket        | Network byte transmission              |
| Scapy         | Construct and dissect packets          |
| dpkt          | Parse network packet structures        |
| Atheris       | Python coverage-guided fuzzing         |

Example: Pack and unpack binary values.

```python
import struct

number = 1024

packed = struct.pack(">I", number)

print("Bytes:", packed)
print("Hex:", packed.hex())
print("Binary:", "".join(
    format(byte, "08b") for byte in packed
))

unpacked = struct.unpack(">I", packed)[0]

print("Recovered:", unpacked)
```

This is particularly useful when building protocol parsers and examining binary file structures.

